APX.ro = {"country":{"name":"Romania","tagline":"Europe's sixth vineyard, ringed by the Carpathians: Fetească in three colours, botrytised Grasă at Cotnari and the Black Sea warmth of Murfatlar.","classification":"Romanian wine law follows the EU: 33 DOCs (*denumire de origine controlată*, the Romanian name for a PDO) and 12 IGs (*indicație geografică*, PGI), plus wine without origin. Several DOCs list sub-denominations that can appear on labels (for example Dealu Mare-Urlați, Dealu Mare-Valea Călugărească, Murfatlar-Medgidia, Târnave-Jidvei). DOC wines may also carry a ripeness level:\n- DOC-CMD (*cules la maturitate deplină*): full ripeness, at least 187 g/l of sugar at harvest\n- DOC-CT (*cules târziu*): late harvest, at least 220 g/l\n- DOC-CIB (*cules la înnobilarea boabelor*): botrytised berry selection, at least 240 g/l\n\nThe national body ONVPV (Oficiul Național al Viei și Produselor Vitivinicole) manages registration and control. Adamclisi (2020) and Însurăței (2018) are the newest DOCs."},"regions":[{"name":"Moldavian Hills","slug":"moldavian-hills","tagline":"Romania's largest wine region, from noble-rot Cotnari to the vast Vrancea vineyards.","ids":["ro-bohotin","ro-cotesti","ro-cotnari","ro-dealu-bujorului","ro-husi","ro-iana","ro-iasi","ro-nicoresti","ro-odobesti","ro-panciu"]},{"name":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","slug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills","tagline":"The southern Carpathian foothills: Dealu Mare's Fetească Neagră and Drăgășani's crisp whites.","ids":["ro-banu-maracine","ro-dealu-mare","ro-dragasani","ro-mehedinti","ro-pietroasa","ro-segarcea","ro-samburesti","ro-stefanesti"]},{"name":"Transylvanian Plateau","slug":"transylvanian-plateau","tagline":"Saxon villages, fortified churches and cool, crisp whites inside the Carpathian arc.","ids":["ro-aiud","ro-alba-iulia","ro-lechinta","ro-sebes-apold","ro-tarnave"]},{"name":"Dobrogea","slug":"dobrogea","tagline":"Between the Danube and the Black Sea: Romania's sunniest, driest vineyards.","ids":["ro-adamclisi","ro-babadag","ro-murfatlar","ro-oltina","ro-sarica-niculitel"]},{"name":"Banat","slug":"banat","tagline":"The south-west: Recaș's big modern winery and Serbian-influenced vineyards on the Danube.","ids":["ro-banat","ro-recas"]},{"name":"Crișana and Maramureș","slug":"cri-ana-and-maramure","tagline":"The western hills toward Hungary: Miniș's Cadarcă and Crișana's white wines.","ids":["ro-crisana","ro-minis"]},{"name":"Danube Terraces and Southern Sands","slug":"danube-terraces-and-southern-sands","tagline":"The Danube floodplain terraces and sand dunes of the south.","ids":["ro-insuratei"]}],"items":{"ro-adamclisi":{"name":"Adamclisi","cat":"DOC","region":"Dobrogea","created":"EU PDO 2020","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Neagră, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Adamclisi became Romania's newest DOC in July 2020, a single commune in southern Dobrogea named for the Roman Tropaeum Traiani monument. Its rules allow only six varieties (Fetească Neagră, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Sauvignon Blanc) at a low 7 t/ha. Alira and Viile Metamorfosis' neighbours have planted modern vineyards here.","producers":["Alira"],"rslug":"dobrogea"},"ro-aiud":{"name":"Aiud","cat":"DOC","region":"Transylvanian Plateau","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Fetească Regală, Fetească Albă, Riesling Italian, Muscat Ottonel, Traminer Roz, Iordana","summary":"Aiud, north of Alba Iulia along the Mureș, makes crisp Transylvanian whites from Fetească Regală, Riesling Italian, Muscat Ottonel, Traminer and the local crossing Iordana. Its six communes include Ocna Mureș, a salt-mining town, and the hills are cool enough for good sparkling base wine.","rslug":"transylvanian-plateau"},"ro-alba-iulia":{"name":"Alba Iulia","cat":"DOC","region":"Transylvanian Plateau","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Riesling Italian, Muscat Ottonel, Traminer Roz, Neuburger, Pinot Gris","summary":"Alba Iulia, around the citadel city on the Mureș where the union of Transylvania with Romania was proclaimed in 1918, is a small white-wine DOC of seven communes including Ighiu and Cricău. Riesling Italian, Fetească Regală, Muscat Ottonel and Traminer dominate.","rslug":"transylvanian-plateau"},"ro-babadag":{"name":"Babadag","cat":"DOC","region":"Dobrogea","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Neagră, Băbească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Aligoté, Fetească Regală, Columna","summary":"Babadag, in northern Dobrogea near the Razim-Sinoe lagoons of the Danube Delta and the Greek ruins of Histria, covers 13 communes making whites and reds in a dry, windy climate. Aligoté and the Murfatlar-bred Columna are local specialities.","rslug":"dobrogea"},"ro-banat":{"name":"Banat","cat":"DOC","region":"Banat","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Cadarcă, Burgund Mare, Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Riesling Italian, Fetească Regală, Majarcă Albă","summary":"The Banat DOC covers the hills of Moldova Nouă and the Dealurile Tirolului near the Danube gorge and the Serbian border. It makes both reds and whites; the area's Czech-founded villages and Danube landscapes give it character.","rslug":"banat"},"ro-banu-maracine":{"name":"Banu Mărăcine","cat":"DOC","region":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Novac, Tămâioasă Românească, Fetească Regală","summary":"Banu Mărăcine is a small DOC around Craiova named after the University of Craiova's teaching and research station; it produces reds and whites from Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet and Tămâioasă.","rslug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills"},"ro-bohotin":{"name":"Bohotin","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["rose","sweet","white"],"grapes":"Busuioacă de Bohotin, Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Aligoté","summary":"Bohotin, south-east of Iași near the Prut, gives its name to Busuioacă de Bohotin, a pink-skinned muscat with a basil-like perfume (*busuioc* means basil), made dry or sweet as a dark rosé. The DOC covers six communes.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-cotesti":{"name":"Cotești","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Fetească Neagră, Băbească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Fetească Albă, Riesling Italian","summary":"Cotești, in southern Vrancea, makes both whites and the region's best-known reds (Fetească Neagră, Băbească Neagră, Merlot) on warmer south-facing slopes; it has high permitted yields and is largely a volume area.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-cotnari":{"name":"Cotnari","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["sweet","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Grasă de Cotnari, Fetească Albă, Tămâioasă Românească, Frâncușă, Busuioacă de Bohotin","summary":"Cotnari, in the hills north-west of Iași, is Romania's most famous historic wine and its answer to Tokaj. The DOC (nine communes) is dedicated to four local whites: Grasă de Cotnari, Fetească Albă, Tămâioasă Românească and Frâncușă, plus the pink Busuioacă de Bohotin. Autumn fogs bring noble rot to Grasă, the same grape as Tokaj's Kövérszőlő, and the best wines are sweet, honeyed and long-lived; residual sugar of about 300 g/l is common, and the 1958 harvest reached about 520 g/l. The vineyards are documented since the 15th century and reached their peak under Peter II, Stephen the Great (1457 to 1504) and Despot Vodă, who founded a Latin school at Cotnari in 1562. The large Cotnari company, privatised after 1989, is one of Romania's biggest single estates, and Casa de Vinuri Cotnari makes dry versions of the same grapes.","terroir":"Clay and sandy loams on the Cotnari hills at 150 to 400 m; autumn fogs encourage botrytis; continental climate with harsh winters.","producers":["Cotnari","Casa de Vinuri Cotnari"],"nerd_note":"Cotnari's Grasă and Tokaj's Kövérszőlő are the same variety: two historic sweet-wine regions on either side of the Carpathians share a grape.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-crisana":{"name":"Crișana","cat":"DOC","region":"Crișana and Maramureș","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Regală, Fetească Albă, Riesling Italian, Mustoasă de Măderat, Pinot Gris, Fetească Neagră","summary":"Crișana covers the north-western hills around Oradea, Diosig, Șimleu Silvaniei and Zalău (23 communes), close to the Hungarian border, making mostly whites from Fetească Regală, Riesling Italian and Mustoasă de Măderat, with some Fetească Neagră.","rslug":"cri-ana-and-maramure"},"ro-dealu-bujorului":{"name":"Dealu Bujorului","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Băbească Neagră, Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Fetească Albă, Aligoté","summary":"Dealu Bujorului ('peony hill') around Târgu Bujor in Galați county is a warm, southern Moldavian DOC with a research station; it makes reds (Băbească Neagră, Fetească Neagră, Merlot) and whites.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-dealu-mare":{"name":"Dealu Mare","cat":"DOC","region":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white","sweet","sparkling"],"grapes":"Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir, Syrah, Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Tămâioasă Românească","summary":"Dealu Mare ('the big hill') is Romania's leading red-wine DOC: a 65 km band of south-facing foothills from Buzău to Ploiești, with 18 to 20 communes including Urlați, Ceptura, Valea Călugărească, Breaza, Merei and Năeni. Sheltered by the Carpathians and open to the southern sun, it ripens Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon and Pinot Noir into rich, plush reds, and its eastern end produces aromatic sweet Tămâioasă. It was where phylloxera first appeared in Romania, at Chitorani, and it is now the base of many of the country's best modern wineries: Davino, LacertA, Serve, Budureasca, Basilescu, Rotenberg, Aurelia Vișinescu, Crama Gîrboiu's neighbours and the historic Halewood estate. Sub-denominations such as Urlați, Valea Călugărească, Ceptura, Merei, Breaza, Zorești and Tohani can appear on labels.","terroir":"South-facing slopes of 130 to 550 m on clays, marls and loess; warm and sunny, sheltered from northern cold.","producers":["Davino","LacertA","Serve","Budureasca","Basilescu","Rotenberg","Aurelia Vișinescu","Tohani"],"nerd_note":"Valea Călugărească ('valley of the monks') houses Romania's national viticulture research institute, which has bred and selected many of the country's clones.","rslug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills"},"ro-dragasani":{"name":"Drăgășani","cat":"DOC","region":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red","rose"],"grapes":"Crâmpoșie Selecționată, Tămâioasă Românească, Sauvignon Blanc, Fetească Regală, Negru de Drăgășani, Novac, Fetească Neagră","summary":"Drăgășani follows the right bank of the Olt in Vâlcea county, a long chain of hills with eleven communes. It is known for crisp, herbal Crâmpoșie Selecționată, aromatic Tămâioasă Românească, Sauvignon Blanc, and two reds bred here, Negru de Drăgășani and Novac. The Știrbey family estate, restituted in 2001 and revived by Ileana Kripp-Costinescu and the Austrian winemaker Oliver Bauer, made the area's modern reputation; Avincis and Bauer are other leading names.","producers":["Prince Știrbey","Avincis","Bauer"],"nerd_note":"Novac and Negru de Drăgășani were both bred at the Drăgășani research station in the 1980s and 1990s by crossing the local Negru Vârtos with Georgia's Saperavi.","rslug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills"},"ro-husi":{"name":"Huși","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Zghihară de Huși, Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Busuioacă de Bohotin, Aligoté, Fetească Neagră","summary":"Huși, near the Prut and the Moldovan border, is famous for Zghihară de Huși, a tart, light local white, and for being a favourite source of wine for the Communist leadership. The DOC covers 17 communes with a long variety list.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-iana":{"name":"Iana","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Aligoté, Busuioacă de Bohotin, Băbească Neagră","summary":"Iana is a small DOC in Vaslui county in central Moldavia (five communes), producing light whites and reds from Fetească Albă, Aligoté, Băbească Neagră and Busuioacă de Bohotin. Most of its wine is sold locally and in bulk; the DOC exists largely to protect a historic name.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-iasi":{"name":"Iași","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Aligoté, Busuioacă de Bohotin, Golia, Arcaș, Fetească Neagră","summary":"The Iași DOC surrounds Moldavia's historic capital, with 13 communes on hills such as Copou and Bucium. It makes aromatic whites and some reds, and is home to university research that bred the Golia and Arcaș varieties.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-insuratei":{"name":"Însurăței","cat":"DOC","region":"Danube Terraces and Southern Sands","created":"EU PDO 2018","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Riesling, Chardonnay, Fetească Neagră, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Însurăței, in Brăila county on the Danube plain, became a DOC in 2018 (four communes). It makes fresh whites and reds from Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Riesling, Chardonnay, Fetească Neagră, Syrah and Cabernet.","rslug":"danube-terraces-and-southern-sands"},"ro-lechinta":{"name":"Lechința","cat":"DOC","region":"Transylvanian Plateau","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Regală, Fetească Albă, Riesling Italian, Sauvignon Blanc, Traminer Roz, Pinot Noir","summary":"Lechința, in Bistrița-Năsăud in northern Transylvania, is a cool white-wine DOC of 17 communes; Liliac's modern winery at Batoș has raised its profile with crisp Fetească Regală, Sauvignon and Pinot Noir.","producers":["Liliac"],"rslug":"transylvanian-plateau"},"ro-mehedinti":{"name":"Mehedinți","cat":"DOC","region":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Fetească Neagră, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Novac, Negru de Drăgășani, Tămâioasă Românească","summary":"Mehedinți, in the far south-west near the Danube's Iron Gates and Drobeta-Turnu Severin, covers about 20 communes including the Corcova and Oprișor areas. Crama Oprișor and Corcova Roy & Dâmboviceanu make some of Romania's most structured reds here.","producers":["Crama Oprișor","Corcova Roy & Dâmboviceanu"],"rslug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills"},"ro-minis":{"name":"Miniș","cat":"DOC","region":"Crișana and Maramureș","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cadarcă, Burgund Mare, Fetească Neagră, Mustoasă de Măderat, Fetească Regală, Riesling Italian, Furmint","summary":"Miniș, in the Zărand hills near Arad, is one of Romania's oldest documented vineyard areas, famous for Cadarcă (Kadarka) red and the local white Mustoasă de Măderat; the DOC covers still, semi-sparkling and quality sparkling wine across nine communes.","rslug":"cri-ana-and-maramure"},"ro-murfatlar":{"name":"Murfatlar","cat":"DOC","region":"Dobrogea","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","sweet","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Muscat Ottonel, Tămâioasă Românească, Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Murfatlar lies just inland from Constanța on the Black Sea, in Dobrogea's chalky, sun-baked hills; it is one of the sunniest vineyard areas in Europe, with very little rain. The DOC (14 communes, with sub-denominations Medgidia, Cernavodă and Megidia) became famous under Communism for rich, late-harvested, often sweet Chardonnay, Pinot Gris and Muscat Ottonel from the big state winery and research station; today the Murfatlar company and smaller estates also make ripe reds from Fetească Neagră, Merlot and Cabernet. The local white crossing Columna was bred at the Murfatlar station.","terroir":"Limestone and chalky loess plateaus; very warm and dry, over 300 days of sun, moderated by the Black Sea.","producers":["Murfatlar","Alira","Viile Metamorfosis","Crama Ceptura (Dobrogea vineyards)"],"rslug":"dobrogea"},"ro-nicoresti":{"name":"Nicorești","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Băbească Neagră, Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Fetească Albă","summary":"Nicorești, in Galați county, is the classic home of Băbească Neagră, a light, sour-cherry red with bright acidity and low tannin that is best drunk young and cool. The DOC (eight communes) also produces Fetească Neagră and whites.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-odobesti":{"name":"Odobești","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Galbenă de Odobești, Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Riesling Italian, Băbească Neagră, Fetească Neagră","summary":"Odobești, in Vrancea, is one of Romania's oldest and most extensive vineyard areas, on the slopes below the Măgura Odobești. It is known for whites, especially the tart local Galbenă de Odobești, Fetească Albă and Riesling Italian, and large-volume production.","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-oltina":{"name":"Oltina","cat":"DOC","region":"Dobrogea","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Oltina lies in south-western Dobrogea near the Danube and the Bulgarian border, with seven communes including Aliman and Ostrov making ripe reds and whites in a warm, dry climate. The nearby Danube lakes temper the summer heat a little.","rslug":"dobrogea"},"ro-panciu":{"name":"Panciu","cat":"DOC","region":"Moldavian Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["sparkling","white"],"grapes":"Fetească Albă, Fetească Regală, Riesling Italian, Muscat Ottonel, Șarba, Galbenă de Odobești, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"Panciu, in northern Vrancea, is known for crisp whites and above all for sparkling wine: the DOC covers quality sparkling and aromatic sparkling (Muscat Ottonel, Tămâioasă, Șarba) as well as still wines, and the town's old cellars, rebuilt after the 1940 and 1977 earthquakes, hold millions of bottles. The area was a front line in World War I (Mărășești, 1917).","rslug":"moldavian-hills"},"ro-pietroasa":{"name":"Pietroasa","cat":"DOC","region":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["sweet","white","red"],"grapes":"Tămâioasă Românească, Grasă de Cotnari, Busuioacă de Bohotin, Fetească Neagră","summary":"Pietroasa, a single commune (Pietroasele) at the eastern end of the Dealu Mare hills in Buzău county, is famous for sweet Tămâioasă Românească grown on limestone-rich slopes; the University of Agronomic Sciences in Bucharest runs a research station here. The village is also where the Pietroasa Treasure, a 4th-century Gothic gold hoard, was found in 1837.","rslug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills"},"ro-recas":{"name":"Recaș","cat":"DOC","region":"Banat","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Fetească Neagră, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Pinot Noir, Syrah, Fetească Regală, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay","summary":"Recaș is a single-commune DOC near Timișoara built around Cramele Recaș, rebuilt from 1998 by Romanian and British partners into one of Romania's largest and most export-focused wineries, with about 1,000 ha. Its vineyards date from the 15th century. It makes everything from inexpensive varietals to the Solo Quinta and Cuvée Uberland ranges, and is the source of much of the Romanian wine seen in UK supermarkets.","producers":["Cramele Recaș"],"rslug":"banat"},"ro-samburesti":{"name":"Sâmburești","cat":"DOC","region":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Pinot Noir, Fetească Neagră, Novac, Negru de Drăgășani","summary":"Sâmburești, in Olt county, was a renowned Cabernet Sauvignon estate in the Communist era, when its reds were among Romania's most prestigious; the DOC covers two communes and is still known for Cabernet and Merlot.","rslug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills"},"ro-sarica-niculitel":{"name":"Sarica Niculițel","cat":"DOC","region":"Dobrogea","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Aligoté, Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Fetească Regală, Rkatsiteli","summary":"Sarica Niculițel, near Tulcea at the gateway to the Danube Delta, is a hilly DOC of 14 communes on the old Măcin hills, known for Aligoté, Fetească Neagră and Merlot.","rslug":"dobrogea"},"ro-sebes-apold":{"name":"Sebeș-Apold","cat":"DOC","region":"Transylvanian Plateau","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Regală, Riesling Italian, Muscat Ottonel, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Pinot Noir, Iordana","summary":"Sebeș-Apold spans the hills between Sebeș and the Saxon villages of Apold and Gârbova; its 12 communes make fresh whites and some reds, and the area's Saxon fortified churches recall its German past.","rslug":"transylvanian-plateau"},"ro-segarcea":{"name":"Segarcea","cat":"DOC","region":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir, Fetească Neagră, Merlot, Tămâioasă Roză, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay","summary":"Segarcea, south of Craiova, was the Crown Domain (Domeniul Coroanei) of the Romanian royal family from 1884 until 1948; nationalised by the Communists, it was revived in the 2000s and now makes Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir, Fetească Neagră and Tămâioasă roză.","producers":["Domeniul Coroanei Segarcea"],"rslug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills"},"ro-stefanesti":{"name":"Ștefănești","cat":"DOC","region":"Muntenia and Oltenia Hills","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Fetească Regală, Fetească Albă, Riesling Italian, Tămâioasă Românească, Burgund Mare, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Ștefănești, near Pitești in Argeș county, hosts a long-standing viticulture research station; the DOC (six communes) produces whites and reds from Fetească Regală, Riesling Italian, Tămâioasă and Burgund Mare (Blaufränkisch), with yields capped at 10 t/ha.","rslug":"muntenia-and-oltenia-hills"},"ro-tarnave":{"name":"Târnave","cat":"DOC","region":"Transylvanian Plateau","created":"EU PDO 2007","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Fetească Regală, Fetească Albă, Riesling Italian, Muscat Ottonel, Sauvignon Blanc, Traminer, Pinot Gris","summary":"Târnave is the largest Transylvanian DOC, covering the valleys of the Târnava Mare and Târnava Mică between Blaj, Mediaș and Sighișoara (about 35 communes). It is cool and high, so the wines are crisp, aromatic whites: Fetească Regală (first noted at Daneș nearby), Fetească Albă, Riesling Italian, Muscat Ottonel, Sauvignon Blanc and Traminer, plus traditional-method sparkling. Jidvei, Romania's largest estate with about 2,500 ha, dominates; its headquarters is the Bethlen-Haller castle at Cetatea de Baltă. Sub-denominations include Jidvei, Mediaș, Blaj, Dealurile Mureșului and Dumbrăveni.","terroir":"Marl and clay hills at 300 to 500 m; cool continental climate, late harvest, high acidity.","producers":["Jidvei","Villa Vinèa","Liliac (Lechința side)","Crama La Salina"],"rslug":"transylvanian-plateau"}}};
